    "assumptions": "Library convention (tax_rates_assumed.md): 401(k) valued net of husband's 24% marginal rate (400,000 x 0.76 = 304,000); brokerage net of 15% LTCG on the 80,000 embedded gain (300,000 - 12,000 = 288,000); residence modeled as sold now at scheduled value: 1,100,000 - 6% selling costs (66,000) - 400,000 mortgage = 634,000, less capital-gain tax on 734,000 gain: (734,000 - 250,000 Section 121 exclusion) x (15% + 3.8% NIIT, applied to the taxable portion per the engagement's stated conservative convention; Wife's actual MAGI may or may not exceed the 200,000 single threshold - unverified, assumption stated) = 90,992, netting 543,008. Cash/vehicles at face. Basis carries over to each spouse under IRC 1041 (irc_1041_121_71.md), so each party's own assets carry their own latent tax. Result: nominal 770,000/770,000 becomes 613,008/662,000 - a 48,992 gap against Wife, driven mainly by the 66,000 selling costs and 91,000 gain tax landing on her side while only one 250,000 exclusion shelters the house. Sensitivity: husband at 12%/32% -> his side 710,000/630,000 (gap 96,992/16,992); without NIIT Wife = 631,400 (gap 30,600); if brokerage gains are short-term (holdings are 'mostly 2024-25 purchases') and taxed at 24%, husband = 654,800. Also unquantified: Wife's side is illiquid (house) vs husband's liquid assets; if Wife stays 3-6 years as she wants, price appreciation enlarges the eventual taxable gain."
